Subject: [PATCH] backend-metal: Apple, not macOS

Metal is Apple's API, not the desktop's. `backend-metal` was gated to
`target_os = "macos"` in four places, and none of them was a statement
about a checked macOS specificity — the module header of
`backend/metal/mod.rs` said so outright: "every `target_os = "macos"` gate
below is a statement of that fact rather than a branch." The fact being
restated was the `compile_error!` in `lib.rs`, and that one was only ever
about there being no host-stub Metal arm. iOS runs the same Metal, on the
same Apple Silicon GPU, through the same `objc` runtime and the same
`metal` crate; it was excluded because nobody had tried, not because
anything here had been found to be desktop-specific.

This is the host half of running the arm64 macOS guest on an iOS device.
The QEMU side already cross-compiles; this is what was left between it and
a frame.

What each gate turned out to be answering, and what it now answers:

1. `lib.rs`'s `compile_error!` — "is there a Metal to reach". The real
   precondition is an Apple platform, so it is keyed on
   `target_vendor = "apple"`. A non-Apple target is still rejected with
   the same message and the same advice.

2. `backend/metal/mod.rs`'s fifteen per-module gates — these asked "did
   this build compile Metal encode", which `feature = "backend-metal"`
   already answers in full once (1) admits every Apple target. They are
   dropped rather than widened: a second gate that can only ever agree
   with the feature is a gate that will one day disagree with it by
   accident. The module header now states the Apple-only fact once,
   without claiming the modules below are branching on it.

3. Every `all(feature = "backend-metal", target_os = "macos")` in the
   crate (122 of them, across `backend/compute_session.rs`,
   `runtime/{compute_exec,compute_session,decode,draw,icb,mipmap}`) —
   same question as (2), same answer, same reduction to the feature
   alone. On `aarch64-apple-darwin` this substitution is an identity:
   `target_os = "macos"` is true there by definition, so the conjunction
   and the bare feature select exactly the same code. The macOS pathway
   is unchanged by construction, not merely by test.

   This subsumes 0003, which had already made this reduction for the five
   `SelectedBackend` match arms where the extra conjunct broke match
   exhaustiveness. The two patches touch disjoint files.

4. `backend/heap_placement.rs` — the one gate here that is genuinely
   about the platform rather than about the build. It asks whether a
   driver exists to answer the guest's `heapTextureSizeAndAlign`
   contract, deliberately independent of which rail is compiled or
   running (see the module header). That question is about the *vendor*:
   iOS ships the same Metal driver on the same GPU, and it is the driver,
   not the host OS, the guest contract is asking about. Keyed on
   `target_vendor = "apple"`, so an iOS host answers the placement query
   from the driver instead of refusing it from nowhere.

`Cargo.toml`'s `[target.'cfg(target_os = "macos")'.dependencies]` — the
`metal`, `objc`, `block`, `foreign-types`, `libc` and
`core-graphics-types` block — moves to `cfg(target_vendor = "apple")` for
the same reason: what those five crates need is an Apple platform.

`window.rs` and the `host-window` feature are untouched. That rail is a
`CAMetalLayer` on an `NSWindow` and genuinely is desktop-shaped; the iOS
embedder is the application itself and takes frames through
`Backend::published_frame_rgba8`, which is a CPU readback and needs no
window. `host-window` stays off there, and the Vulkan rail is untouched
throughout.

## No Metal API difference had to be resolved, and why that is the finding

The expectation going in was that `device.rs` / `raw_metal.rs` would need
real work for iOS — `MTLStorageModeManaged` has no iOS spelling, argument
buffer tiers differ, feature-set queries differ. None of it materialised,
and the reason is structural rather than lucky: this backend was already
written to the unified-memory Apple Silicon subset, which is what iOS
*is*.

  - Every allocation in the rail is `Shared` (or `Private`). There is no
    `Managed` anywhere under `backend/metal/`, so the storage-mode
    difference has no site to appear at.
  - No `synchronizeResource:`, `synchronizeTexture:…`, `didModifyRange:`
    or `setPurgeableState:` — the managed-memory selector family is
    absent for the same reason.
  - No `MTLFeatureSet`, `supportsFamily:` or `argumentBuffersSupport`
    query exists in the rail; placement policy is not derived from a
    device class.
  - "IOSurface" throughout this crate names the *guest's* surface pages,
    not the host framework. No host IOSurface, CoreVideo, CoreGraphics
    or AppKit symbol is reached outside `window.rs`.
  - `runtime.rs`'s `newBufferWithBytesNoCopy` path already measures the
    host page with `sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE)` at run time and const-asserts
    the guest arm64e page (16 KiB) against the Apple Silicon page. Both
    hold on iOS unchanged; the assertion is checked by every compile,
    this one included.
  - `constants.rs`'s argument-table caps (31 buffers, 128 textures, 16
    samplers, 8 colour RTs) are the Apple-family limits from family 6
    upward, which is every device that could host this.

So the nine compile errors this port produced were all in the crate's own
glue, all of category (3), and none in `backend/metal/` itself.

## Verified, and not

Verified: `cargo build --release --target aarch64-apple-ios
--no-default-features --features backend-metal -p reims-vgpu` completes
clean; the emitted objects carry `LC_VERSION_MIN_IPHONEOS` (device, not
simulator). `cargo clippy` on the same target adds no new warning. On
`aarch64-apple-darwin` the suite is unchanged: 1391 pass, and the two
failures present are both reproduced identically on a pristine baseline
of the same tree — `runtime::spirv_bind::more_tests::a_module_a_
validator_rejects_never_reaches_a_driver`, and fifteen
`runtime::icb::tests` mesh cases that abort inside Metal validation with
"Use of mesh shader pipelines in indirect command buffers is not
supported by this device", which is a limit of the host GPU this ran on.
The other 47 icb cases pass.

Measured on a device after the fact, with `tools/ios-metallib-probe` on
an Apple A16 GPU: (a) below is answered yes, and (d) is answered no. The
remaining two still need a booted VM, which this pathway has never had —
it is not in the Supported pathways table:

  a. ~~Whether the guest's MTLB libraries load on an iOS driver.~~
     **ANSWERED — YES.** Measured on an Apple A16 GPU with
     `tools/ios-metallib-probe`: a metallib built by the macOS Metal
     compiler loads on an iOS device as-is, exposing every function.
     No platform restamp is needed; the probe tries one anyway and it
     also loads, so the stamp is not enforced in either direction. This
     was the risk that decided between a desktop and a blank screen, and
     it is closed.

     A failure here would still surface as
     `metal_function_library_create_failed reason=… mtlb_len=N`, which
     remains the slug to grep for in a run that boots but never draws.

  b. **Whether `heapTextureSizeAndAlign` returns the same placement on
     iOS as on macOS.** Point (4) assumes the guest ABI's answer is the
     GPU's and not the host OS's. Same silicon, same driver family, and
     a differing answer would be a host implementation detail leaking
     into a guest contract — but it is an assumption, and a wrong one
     would corrupt heap placement silently rather than refuse.

  c. **Device-capability aborts.** The fifteen mesh-in-ICB failures above
     are the shape of this: Metal answers an unsupported combination
     with a validation `abort()`, not a status this device can decline.
     Which combinations an iOS GPU refuses that a Mac accepts is not
     enumerable from here.

  d. **BC pixel formats — MEASURED ABSENT, and this is now the largest
     known gap.** The guess recorded in an earlier draft of this patch,
     that BC arrives with Apple family 7 (A14/M1), is wrong. The probe
     reports `supportsBCTextureCompression == false` on an Apple A16 GPU
     that advertises `apple6 apple7 apple8`, while an M1 Mac advertising
     only `apple6 apple7` reports `true`. BC therefore tracks **Mac**,
     not the family ordinal, and no iPhone has it regardless of vintage.

     A macOS guest believes it is on a Mac and will send BC textures.
     The Vulkan rail already survives this: `block_compressed` in
     `runtime/draw/texture_view.rs` is a measured capability, and a host
     that clears it loses the texture with a typed reason. **The Metal
     rail has no counterpart** — `backend/metal/format.rs` names no BC
     format and gates nothing, because on every Mac this rail has ever
     run on the answer was yes.

     Left untouched here deliberately: adding the gate is a device-model
     behaviour change, not part of making the rail compile, and it
     belongs in its own patch with its own reasoning. What this patch
     owes is the statement that the gap exists and is real rather than
     hypothetical. Note the failure mode is the one from (c) — Metal
     refuses an unsupported format by aborting the process, not by
     returning nil — so the gate is worth having before a long guest run.

No stub or placeholder was left in place of an implementation: nothing in
this patch replaces a real path with a degraded one. What is unfinished
is verification, not code.

